{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794406302,"nonce":"p1SgKWxIEPgPOWddA7Nu4Adl+jk=","message":"Delivery was reworked the same day, after the user pointed out that cooperation across the native boundary is where reactive apps get tricky, and that hxn has to suit any kind of Haxe app, not one with a particular loop.\n\nWhat was wrong with the first version: every `then`, `listen` and `follow` had a helper thread of its own posting to `Thread.current().events`. So an app with a frame loop got nothing unless it turned that loop, results of different kinds could arrive out of order, values set together arrived as separate updates, and a set made through a binding was not visible in its signal until a later turn.\n\nWhat replaced it:\n\n- Rust: `hxn_future::outbox`, one queue per library. A future, stream or signal the host watches notes itself there when it has something new, once until the host has looked, so the queue is bounded by what is watched and unwatched results queue nothing.\n- Haxe: `hxn.Inbox`, one per library, drains that queue and calls the watchers for each id, in order, inside one `Reactive.batch`. `Future.then`, `Stream.listen` and `Reactive.follow` are watchers on it.\n- `hxn.Loop`: by default an inbox is drained from the event loop of the thread that first asked, by one helper thread a library that blocks on the outbox (a loop turn each on a runtime without threads). An app with its own loop sets `Loop.manual` and calls `Loop.sync()` where it wants deliveries; the Haxe event loop is then neither used nor kept alive.\n- `Inbox.follow` takes the sink as a parameter, so an app with a graph of its own (ashui's is in native code) can feed its own signals. `Reactive.follow` is that with an hxn `Signal` as the sink.\n- Write-through is the binding's: the fixture's `Gauge` keeps its followed signal and sets it when a set goes through it, so code reads its own writes. The later delivery brings the same value and changes nothing.\n\nTested in the fixture's `frames()` with `Loop.manual`: nothing arrives before `sync`; two futures and a stream then arrive in the order the library produced them; a frame loop of `sync` and `sleep` finishes stepped work; two values set in a batch are one rerun and the delivery adds none. The event-loop tests (`then`, `listen`, followed signals) run on the default driver. All of it on Ash in hybrid, jit and interp modes, Ash wasm, stock HashLink, hxcpp and Emscripten.\n\nStill open:\n\n- Two values the library sets together can still be split by a delivery landing between the two sets. A library-side transaction that publishes several changes at once would close that.\n- A library's results are delivered on one thread, the one that first asked or the one that calls `sync`. A `then` registered from another Haxe thread is delivered there too.\n- Inside a delivery, effects rerun when the whole delivery ends, since it is one batch. A signal's value is current at once.\n- A stream is noted once however many items it holds, so its place in the order is that of its first unread item.\n- The generator does not emit any of this yet.","files":null}]}